{"name":"Splatter Movie","hex":"#d01a2c","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"splatter","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An uneven shape or mess created by something dispersing on impact.","A genre of gory horror."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To splash; to scatter; to land or strike in an uneven, distributed mess.","To cause (something) to splatter."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/splatter"},{"term":"movie","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A recorded sequence of images displayed on a screen at a rate sufficiently fast to create the appearance of motion; a film; a video.","A professionally made motion picture of a substantive narrative work, typically around 1 to 3 hours long, typically intended for cinema, art, entertainment, and a widespread audience; a feature film, theatrical film."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/movie"}],"entries":[{"title":"Splatter film","blocks":[{"text":"A splatter film is a subgenre of horror film that deliberately focuses on graphic portrayals of gore and graphic violence. These films, usually through the use of special effects, display a fascination with the vulnerability of the human body and the theatricality of its mutilation. The term \"splatter cinema\" was coined by George A. Romero to describe his film Dawn of the Dead, though Dawn of the Dead is generally considered by critics as possessing higher aspiration rather than simply being exploitative for its own sake."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/ba/Blood-feast.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Splatter_film"},{"title":"Film","blocks":[{"text":"A film, movie, or motion picture is a work of visual art that simulates experiences and otherwise communicates ideas, stories, perceptions, emotions, or atmosphere through the use of moving images that are generally, since the 1930s, synchronized with sound and sometimes feature other sensory stimuli."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Le_Voyage_dans_la_Lune_%281902%29.webm/330px--Le_Voyage_dans_la_Lune_%281902%29.webm.jpg","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Film"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/9f/CannibalHolocaustimpale.jpg/330px-CannibalHolocaustimpale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/9f/CannibalHolocaustimpale.jpg/330px-CannibalHolocaustimpale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The 1980 mockumentary Cannibal Holocaust, an influential example of splatter cinema","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACannibalHolocaustimpale.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/83/Hostelchairshot.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/83/Hostelchairshot.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Bijou Phillips in Eli Roth's 2007 film Hostel: Part II, portraying a woman being tortured","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHostelchairshot.PNG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Prof._Stampfer%27s_Stroboscopische_Scheibe_No._X.gif/500px-Prof._Stampfer%27s_Stroboscopische_Scheibe_No._X.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Prof._Stampfer%27s_Stroboscopische_Scheibe_No._X.gif/500px-Prof._Stampfer%27s_Stroboscopische_Scheibe_No._X.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Animated GIF of Prof. Stampfer's Stroboscopische Scheibe No. X (Trentsensky & Vieweg 1833)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AProf._Stampfer's_Stroboscopische_Scheibe_No._X.